>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lithium · Chemistry · Last reviewed 2026-09-18</p>
      <h1>LCO Battery: The Original Lithium Chemistry</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Lithium cobalt oxide is the chemistry that started the lithium-ion era — and three decades later it is
        still the standard inside the device you are probably reading this on, for reasons no newer chemistry
        has been able to beat.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>At a glance:</strong> LCO&rsquo;s layered oxide cathode delivers roughly 3.6–3.7V nominal voltage and
        the highest energy density per gram among mainstream cathodes — which is why it dominates phones,
        laptops and wearables. Its costs are the mirror image: cycle life is short (on the order of 300–500
        full cycles for consumer cells), and cobalt is typically around 60% of the cathode by weight, making
        LCO the most cobalt-intensive commercial chemistry and the most exposed to cobalt price swings.
      </div>

      <h2>What makes LCO the energy-density champion</h2>
      <p>
        The layered LiCoO&sb2; structure lets lithium ions shuttle out and back between stacked oxygen planes
        with a high operating voltage and good rate capability in a small, dense package. For a phone that is
        charged daily and replaced every two to three years, energy per gram matters far more than cycle count
        or materials cost — and that is precisely LCO&rsquo;s trade: it optimizes the axis consumer electronics
        actually rewards.
      </p>

      <h2>LCO&rsquo;s limits, in numbers</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>LCO (typical consumer cells)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Nominal voltage</td><td>~3.6–3.7V (high-voltage variants charge to 4.45V+)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cell energy density</td><td>~150–200 Wh/kg, format-dependent</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cycle life</td><td>~300–500 full cycles (typical, rate- and voltage-dependent)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thermal class</td><td>Layered oxide — less stable than olivine, protection circuits mandatory</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cobalt content</td><td>~60% of cathode weight (typical)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>An original calculation: cobalt exposure per battery</h2>
      <p>
        A phone cell holds roughly 15 Wh. At ~60% cobalt in the cathode and a working cathode share of roughly
        1/3 of cell weight, that cell carries on the order of 3 g of cobalt — a few cents at 2024–2026 cobalt
        prices, but it is the same arithmetic that scales painfully: a chemistry built on the most volatile
        battery metal stays cheap only while cobalt stays cheap. When cobalt prices spiked in past cycles, LCO
        cells absorbed the shock directly, which is why every major cell maker invested in cobalt reduction —
        through high-voltage formulations that extract more energy per gram of cobalt, not through abandoning
        the chemistry.
      </p>

      <h2>Why LCO never went to cars</h2>
      <p>
        EVs demand thousands of cycles and strict cost per kilowatt-hour; LCO offers hundreds of cycles and the
        industry&rsquo;s highest cobalt bill. The chemistry that fits in a pocket fails on a drivetrain — which is
        why vehicles moved to <a href="/lithium/lfp-vs-nmc-vs-lto/">LFP and NMC</a>, while LCO stayed exactly
        where its trade makes sense.
      </p>

      <h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Charge-voltage ceiling (4.40V vs 4.45V+ high-voltage variants) — energy density and calendar life both move with it.</li>
        <li>Cycle-life test conditions — rate, depth of discharge and temperature all shift the 300–500 cycle figure.</li>
        <li>Cobalt sourcing documentation — the chemistry&rsquo;s cost and compliance risk live in that one metal.</li>
        <li>Protection-circuit configuration and safety certifications for the assembled pack.</li>
        <li>Calendar-aging data — consumer cells age on the shelf as well as in use.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-item"><h3>Why is LCO used in phones but not EVs?</h3><p>LCO wins on energy density per gram, which is what a phone or laptop needs, but its cycle life is short and its cobalt content is high — both disqualify it for electric vehicles, where LFP and NMC dominate.</p></div>
      <div class="faq-item"><h3>Is LCO being replaced?</h3><p>Not in consumer electronics, where LCO remains the standard cathode — cobalt reduction happens through doping and high-voltage formulations rather than chemistry replacement, while cobalt-free chemistries took over vehicles and storage instead.</p></div>
      <div class="faq-item"><h3>How much cobalt is in an LCO battery?</h3><p>Cobalt is typically around 60% of the cathode material by weight in LCO, which makes the chemistry the most cobalt-intensive of the commercial cathodes — and the most exposed to cobalt price cycles.</p></div>

      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p><strong>My position:</strong> LCO is not a legacy chemistry being phased out — it is a specialist that never pretended to be a generalist, and it will hold the pocket-electronics niche until a chemistry beats it at its own metric: energy per gram at small scale.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The cobalt critique is real but priced in; buyers who understand the trade buy LCO deliberately, and buyers who do not are the ones surprised by cycle life. Understanding beats substitution here.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>Editorial interpretation, not purchasing advice.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Typical values compiled from cell-manufacturer datasheets and cathode-material publications (2024–2026); ranges stated as typical — see the platform <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>.</p>

